issues.description:
**Reported:** 2026-08-16 UAT (Kyle) — "single and multi select should be clickable, right now clicking does not show a dialog but takes us to the entity detail."

## Pain point
In grid, list and board rows the chip for a select / multiselect / workflow field is a small control inside a much larger cell. The row's click handler only suppressed navigation when the click landed *exactly* on a control, so every click on the surrounding cell padding — the majority of the cell's area — routed to `openEntity` and yanked the user off to the entity page instead of opening the chooser.

## Reproduction
1. Open `Development/Issue` in Table view.
2. Click the Severity cell anywhere except directly on the `Medium` chip (e.g. 6px inside the cell's right edge).
3. The app navigates to `#/entity/<id>`; no picker opens.

Measured before the fix on row 1's Severity cell: cell width 77.7px, chip width 69.7px starting 4px in — but on wider tables the dead zone is far larger (the same cell measures 180px wide with a ~70px chip once the table has room). Live check:

```
click at (cell.right - 6, cell.middle)
before: { pop: false, hash: "#/entity/ef7a2df0-…" }   ← navigated
after:  { pop: true,  hash: "#/table/b3ca39b7-…" }    ← picker opened, no navigation
```

## Root cause
Three row surfaces each carried a copy of the same guard:

```js
if (e.target.closest('input,select,textarea,button,a,label,.ms-box,.chip')) return;
openEntity(item.id);
```

The guard is control-shaped, not cell-shaped: a click on the `<td>` itself matches nothing and falls through to navigation. The chip picker itself worked correctly the whole time (`chipPicker` stops propagation), which is why the bug reads as "sometimes it works" — it worked only on a direct chip hit.

## Fix
Cells are tagged by field-type group, and the row handler routes a click on a picker cell to that cell's control rather than to the entity:

```js
const PICKER_FIELD_TYPES = ['select', 'multiselect', 'workflow'];
const READONLY_FIELD_TYPES = ['lookup', 'rollup', 'formula', 'document'];

function openCellPicker(cell) {
  const trigger = cell.querySelector('.chip-trigger');
  if (trigger) return trigger.click();
  const sel = cell.querySelector('select');
  if (!sel) return;
  sel.focus();
  try { sel.showPicker?.(); } catch { /* not user-activated — focus is enough */ }
}

function rowClickTarget(e) {
  if (e.target.closest('input,select,textarea,button,a,label,.ms-box,.chip')) return 'ignore';
  return e.target.closest('.cell-pick');
}
```

All three row surfaces (grid `<tr>`, `.list-row`, board card) now share:

```js
const pick = rowClickTarget(e);
if (pick === 'ignore') return;
if (pick) return openCellPicker(pick);
openEntity(item.id);
```

The multiselect `+` control is still a native `<select>`, so it opens via `showPicker()` where available and falls back to focus. `.wv-grid td.cell-pick { cursor: pointer }` advertises the enlarged hit target.

## Test coverage
`test/ui-contract.test.mjs`:
- `picker-type cells are tagged so the row handler can route clicks` — `PICKER_FIELD_TYPES` must list select, multiselect and workflow, and the `cell-pick` class must exist.
- `row click on a picker cell opens the picker instead of the entity` — asserts **all three** row surfaces route through `rowClickTarget`/`openCellPicker`, and that the count of `openEntity(item.id)` call sites equals the count of routed handlers, so a fourth row surface cannot be added without routing.
- `picker cells advertise themselves as clickable` — `.wv-grid td.cell-pick` declares a cursor.

**Reported:** 2026-08-16 UAT (Kyle) — "new space dialog does not match design style, also clicking multiple new spaces or tables makes many blank unfilled fields."

## Pain point
Two problems in the same flow.

1. **Stacking.** `inlineNameInput` appended a fresh input on every click with no dedupe — `foot.append(…)` for a space, `spaceRow.after(…)` for a table. Five clicks on "+ New space" left five empty inputs in the sidebar, and there was no way to dismiss them except pressing Escape in each one or reloading. Verified: five clicks produced five inputs before, one after.
2. **Off-design.** The input was a stock `form-control form-control-sm` dropped into a list of `.nav-db` rows — different height, different type scale, no indication that Enter commits or Escape cancels.

There was also a **third, invisible** copy of the same feature: `newSpaceModal()` and `newTableModal()` existed in `app.js` as modal-based versions of the identical action, styled completely differently — and were never called from anywhere. The same action had two competing designs, one of them dead.

## Fix
Single-instance, self-cancelling, styled as a nav row:

```js
function inlineNameInput(placeholder, onCommit) {
  document.querySelectorAll('.nav-inline-add').forEach((n) => n.remove());
  const input = el('input', { class: 'form-control form-control-sm nav-inline-add', placeholder });
  const cancel = () => { input.remove(); renderNav(); };
  input.addEventListener('keydown', async (e) => {
    if (e.key === 'Escape') return cancel();
    if (e.key !== 'Enter' || !input.value.trim()) return;
    input.disabled = true;                       // no double-submit
    try { await onCommit(input.value.trim()); } catch (err) { input.disabled = false; toast(err.message, true); }
  });
  input.addEventListener('blur', () => { if (!input.disabled && !input.value.trim()) cancel(); });
  requestAnimationFrame(() => input.focus());
  return input;
}
```

Opening a create input anywhere clears any other one, an abandoned empty input removes itself on blur, and committing locks the field so a double Enter cannot create twice.

`.nav-inline-add` now matches a `.nav-db` row — same 5px/8px padding, inherited type scale, primary-coloured border to show it is active.

`modal()` had the same latent bug (a second open stacked another backdrop and another set of blank inputs), so it now removes any existing `#modal-back` first and closes on Escape. The two dead modal functions were deleted.

## Test coverage
`test/ui-contract.test.mjs`:
- `only one inline create input can be open at a time` — the clear-others call, the blur cancel and the disable-on-commit.
- `only one modal can be open at a time` — the pre-open removal and Escape.
- `spaces and tables have exactly one create flow` — the modal variants must stay deleted and the inline input must stay styled.

